Hello Colin,

 >> In KIP-944, the callback thread can only delegate to another thread=20
after reading from and writing to a threadlocal variable, providing the=20
barriers right there.

 > I don't see any documentation that accessing thread local variables=20
provides a total store or load barrier. Do you have such documentation?=20
It seems like if this were the case, we could eliminate volatile=20
variables from most of the code base.

Now I was imprecise. The thread-locals are only somewhat involved. In=20
the KIP proposal the callback thread reads an access key from a=20
thread-local variable. It then needs to pass that access key to another=20
thread, which then can set it on its own thread-local variable. The act=20
of passing a value from one thread to another implies that a memory=20
barrier needs to be passed. However, this is all not so relevant since=20
there is no need to pass the access key back when the other thread is don=
e.

But now I think about it a bit more, the locking mechanism runs in a=20
synchronized block. If I remember correctly this should be enough to=20
pass read and write barriers.

 >> In the current implementation the consumer is also invoked from=20
random threads. If it works now, it should continue to work.
 > I'm not sure what you're referring to. Can you expand on this?

Any invocation of the consumer (e.g. method poll) is not from a thread=20
managed by the consumer. This is what I was assuming you meant with the=20
term 'random thread'.

 > Hmm, not sure what you mean by "cooperate with blocking code." If you=20
have 10 green threads you're multiplexing on to one CPU thread, and that=20
CPU thread gets blocked because of what one green thread is doing, the=20
other 9 green threads are blocked too, right? I guess it's "just" a=20
performance problem, but it still seems like it could be a serious one.

There are several ways to deal with this. All async runtimes I know=20
(Akka, Zio, Cats-effects) support this by letting you mark a task as=20
blocking. The runtime will then either schedule it to another=20
thread-pool, or it will grow the thread-pool to accommodate. In any case=20
'the other 9 green threads' will simply be scheduled to another real=20
thread. In addition, some of these runtimes detect long running tasks=20
and will reschedule waiting tasks to another thread. This is all a bit=20
off topic though.

 > I don't see why this has to be "inherently multi-threaded." Why can't=20
we have the other threads report back what messages they've processed to=20
the worker thread. Then it will be able to handle these callbacks=20
without involving the other threads.

Please consider the context which is that we are running inside the=20
callback of the rebalance listener. The only way to execute something=20
and also have a timeout on it is to run the something on another thread.

Kind regards,
 =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 Erik.


