Hi Philip,

I have been scanning through=20
https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/Consumer+threading+refa=
ctor+design=20
and KIP-848 and from this I understand that the kafka consumer API will=20
not change.

Perhaps the refactoring and/or KIP-848 is a good opportunity to improve=20
the API somewhat. In this email I explain why and also give a rough idea=20
what that could look like.

In the current API, the rebalance listener callback gives the user a=20
chance to commit all work in progress before a partition is actually=20
revoked and assigned to another consumer.

While the callback is doing all this, the main user thread is not able=20
to process new incoming data. So the rebalance listener affects latency=20
and throughput for non-revoked partitions during a rebalance.

In addition, I feel that doing a lot of stuff /in/ a callback is always=20
quite awkward. Better only use it to trigger some processing elsewhere.

Therefore, I would like to propose a new API that does not have these=20
problems and is easy to use (and I hope still easy to implement). In my=20
ideal world, poll is the only method that you need. Lets call it poll2=20
(to do: come up with a less crappy name). Poll2 returns more than just=20
the polled records, it will also contain newly assigned partitions,=20
partitions that will be revoked during the next call to poll2,=20
partitions that were lost, and perhaps it will even contain the offsets=20
committed so far.

The most important idea here is that partitions are not revoked=20
immediately, but in the next call to poll2.

With this API, a user can commit offsets at their own pace during a=20
rebalance. Optionally, for the case that processing of data from the=20
to-be-revoked partition is stil ongoing, we allow the user to postpone=20
the actual revocation in the next poll, so that polling can continue for=20
other partitions.

Since we are no longer blocking the main user thread, partitions that=20
are not revoked can be processed at full speed.

Removal of the rebalance listener also makes the API safer; there is no=20
more need for the thread-id check (nor KIP-944) because, concurrent=20
invocations are simply no longer needed. (Of course, if backward=20
compatibility is a goal, not all of these things can be done.)

Curious to your thoughts and kind regards,
 =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 Erik.

--=20
Erik van Oosten
e.vanoosten@grons.nl
https://day-to-day-stuff.blogspot.com


