Subject: [jira] [Created] (KAFKA-15230) ApiVersions data between controllers
 is not reliable
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394

David Arthur created KAFKA-15230:
------------------------------------

             Summary: ApiVersions data between controllers is not reliable
                 Key: KAFKA-15230
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-15230
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
            Reporter: David Arthur


While testing ZK migrations, I noticed a case where the controller was not =
starting the migration due to the missing ApiVersions data from other contr=
ollers. This was unexpected because the quorum was running and the follower=
s were replicating the metadata log as expected. After examining a heap dum=
p of the leader, it was in fact the case that the ApiVersions map of NodeAp=
iVersions was empty.

=C2=A0

After further investigation and offline discussion with [~jsancio], we real=
ized that after the initial leader election, the connection from the Raft l=
eader to the followers will become idle and eventually timeout and close. T=
his causes NetworkClient to purge the NodeApiVersions data for the closed c=
onnections.

=C2=A0

There are two main side effects of this behavior:=C2=A0

1) If migrations are not started within the idle timeout period (10 minutes=
, by default), then they will not be able to be started. After this timeout=
 period, I was unable to restart the controllers in such a way that the lea=
der had active connections with all followers.

2) Dynamically updating features, such as "metadata.version", is not guaran=
teed to be safe

=C2=A0

There is a partial workaround for the migration issue. If we set "
connections.max.idle.ms" to -1, the Raft leader will never disconnect from =
the followers. However, if a follower restarts, the leader will not re-esta=
blish a connection.
=C2=A0
The feature update issue has no safe workarounds.



