Hello Chris,

Thanks for elaborating Matthias' words. Apparently the use case=20
description is too terse. Indeed, that is not FUD and that is something=20
I can work with.

 > It's also worth mentioning that what's proposed in the KIP is only=20
blocked by the private access modifier on the KafkaConsumer::acquire and=20
KafkaConsumer::release methods. If we upgraded the visibility of these=20
methods from private to protected, it would be possible for subclasses=20
to implement the proposal in KIP-944, without any KIPs or other changes=20
to the official Java clients library.

That is absolutely brilliant! Since I am pretty sure I am using the=20
consumer correctly, I could replace acquire and release with an empty=20
method body and be done.

/Is making acquire and release protected something that other people can=20
live with?/
If yes, I will create a new PR with just that change.

