Hello Xiangyuan LI,

I am not familiar with coroutines, nor with Kotlin. You will have to=20
work with the documentation:=20
https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coroutines-overview.html

However, I am familiar with Zio and Cats-effects (both Scala libraries).=20
In both Zio and Cats-effects one creates effects (aka workflows) which=20
are descriptions of a computation. For example, when executing the Scala=20
code `val effect =3D ZIO.attempt(println("Hello world!"))` one creates=20
only a description; it does not print anything yet. The language to=20
describe these effects is rich enough to describe entire applications=20
including things like concurrency. In fact, the language is so rich,=20
that it is the most convenient way that I know to safely write highly=20
concurrent and async applications. For many developer teams the=20
performance penalty (which is real but not big) is worth it.

To execute a Zio or Cats effect one gives it to the runtime. The runtime=20
then schedules the work on one of the threads in its thread-pool. Zio,=20
nor Cats-effects supports running an effect on the thread that manages=20
the thread-pool.

I hope this clear enough.

Kind regards,
 =C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0 Erik.


